Flo and Clue have the strongest baseline: both are widely recognized cycle trackers, and Flo has especially high global visibility. Stardust has sharper triggers in this set—privacy, women-led design, moon phases, and its explicit-name query. Ovia is more likely to appear for trying to conceive, pregnancy, and postpartum prompts. Eve by Glow has fewer strong query-specific triggers and is less prominent in current general recommendations. [Oxford research on menstrual-app downloads](https://www.ndph.ox.ac.uk/news/global-trends-for-menstrual-tracking-app-use), [Flo privacy features](https://flo.health/privacy-portal), [Clue](https://helloclue.com/), [Stardust features](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.stardust.app), [Ovia](https://www.oviahealth.com/ovia-app/), [Eve by Glow](https://wp.glowing.com/eve-app)

I included the nine unrelated time-tracking questions in the denominator, where these apps are unlikely to be named. The estimates also account for explicit-name prompts: “not Flo” can still mention Flo, and “like Flo” may name it as the comparison.

{"Flo":60,"Clue":49,"Stardust":31,"Eve by Glow":10,"Ovia":18}

**Bottom-up:** Flo and Clue are the most likely names in broad “best period tracker” answers, and Flo is guaranteed to be mentioned in “not Flo” and “like Flo” queries. Clue also fits science-based, birth-control logging, conception, and perimenopause prompts. Stardust has several strong query-level triggers: its name appears explicitly, and its moon-phase, privacy, women-founded, and partner-sharing features match other prompts. Ovia fits conception, pregnancy, postpartum, and perimenopause questions; Eve by Glow has fewer distinctive matches and is less likely to appear in broad recommendations. The nine time-tracking prompts and the dog-tracking prompt are mostly off-topic for these apps.

**Top-down:** Flo and Clue should have the highest rates because they are widely recognized period trackers. Search evidence supports that broad prominence: a global study identifies Flo and Clue among the dominant apps, while their own product pages emphasize cycle tracking and related life stages. Stardust’s overall rate is lower, but its specific feature matches lift it well above Eve and Ovia. Flo’s privacy positioning, Clue’s sharing and conception features, Stardust’s moon and partner features, and Ovia’s postpartum and perimenopause support inform the query-level adjustments. [Global study of menstrual-tracking app downloads](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11585065/), [Flo privacy features](https://flo.health/privacy-portal), [Clue features](https://helloclue.com/), [Stardust features](https://www.stardust.app/app-features), [Ovia app](https://www.oviahealth.com/ovia-app/), [Eve by Glow](https://wp.glowing.com/eve-app)

